    "question": {
      "text": "What is the probability that a quadratic equation $ax^2+bx+c=0$ has equal roots if $a$, $b$ and $c$ are distinct and are taken from $\\{1,2,3,4,6,8,9\\}$?",
      "type": "MCQ",
      "options": [
        {
          "label": "A",
          "text": "$\\frac1{35}$"
        },
        {
          "label": "B",
          "text": "$\\frac2{35}$"
        },
        {
          "label": "C",
          "text": "$\\frac1{105}$"
        },
        {
          "label": "D",
          "text": "$\\frac2{105}$"
        }
      ],
      "marks": null,
      "negativeMarks": null,
      "difficulty": null
    },
    "answer": {
      "correctOption": "C",
      "correctText": "$\\frac1{105}$",
      "explanation": "Considering different values of $a$, $b$ and $c$ from the set $\\{1,2,3,4,6,8,9\\}$, we get different quadratic equations.\nAs $a$, $b$ and $c$ are distinct, ${}^7P_3=210$ different quadratic equations can be formed.\nFor the quadratic equation $ax^2+bx+c=0$ to have equal roots, $b^2=4ac$.\nThe possible combinations of $a$, $b$ and $c$ respectively are $(1,6,9)$ and $(9,6,1)$.\nHence favourable cases are 2.\n$$\\text{Required probability}=\\frac{2}{210}=\\frac1{105}.$$\nChoice (C)",
      "status": "confirmed"
    },
