          "label": "A",
          "text": "$\\frac{10}{17}$"
        },
        {
          "label": "B",
          "text": "$\\frac{7}{17}$"
        },
        {
          "label": "C",
          "text": "$\\frac{7}{10}$"
        },
        {
          "label": "D",
          "text": "$\\frac3{10}$"
        }
      ],
      "marks": null,
      "negativeMarks": null,
      "difficulty": null
    },
    "answer": {
      "correctOption": "B",
      "correctText": "$\\frac{7}{17}$",
      "explanation": "The probability of picking up an orange ball is $\\frac3{10}$ while not picking up an orange ball is $\\frac7{10}$.\nWe compute the probability of Arpit (the beginner) winning the game.\nLet $A$ and $B$ be the events of Arpit and Bipin picking up an orange ball respectively.\nThe winning sequence of Arpit can be $A,\\ A\\bar B A,\\ A\\bar B A\\bar B A,\\ldots$.\nAs the above sequence indicates, Arpit may pick an orange ball right in the 1st trial with a probability of $\\frac3{10}$, or in the third trial with probability $\\left(\\frac7{10}\\right)^1\\frac3{10}$, or in the fifth trial with probability $\\left(\\frac7{10}\\right)^2\\frac3{10}$ and so on.\n$$P(A)=\\frac3{10}+\\left(\\frac7{10}\\right)^1\\frac3{10}+\\left(\\frac7{10}\\right)^2\\frac3{10}+\\cdots$$\n$$=\\frac{\\frac3{10}}{1-\\left(\\frac7{10}\\right)^2}=\\frac{30}{51}=\\frac{10}{17}.$$\nProbability of Bipin winning is the same as probability of Arpit losing, i.e.,\n$$P(B)=P(\\bar A)=1-\\frac{10}{17}=\\frac7{17}.$$\nChoice (B)",
      "status": "confirmed"
