    "question": {
      "text": "$X$ and $Y$ are two independent normal variates with means 3, 6 and variances, 1, 9 respectively. Find the value of $k$ such that $P(X+Y\\le k)=P(9X-Y\\ge2k)$.",
      "type": "MCQ",
      "options": [
        {
          "label": "A",
          "text": "$9.3$"
        },
        {
          "label": "B",
          "text": "$9.6$"
        },
        {
          "label": "C",
          "text": "$8.6$"
        },
        {
          "label": "D",
          "text": "$10.3$"
        }
      ],
      "marks": null,
      "negativeMarks": null,
      "difficulty": null
    },
    "answer": {
      "correctOption": "B",
      "correctText": "$9.6$",
      "explanation": "Given mean of $X=3$, variance of $X=1$, mean of $Y=6$, variance of $Y=9$.\n$X=N(3,1)$, $Y=N(6,3)$ and $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\nLet $u=x+y$ and $v=9x-y$.\nThen $u,v$ are also normal variates.\n$$U=x+y=N(3+6,1+9)=N(9,10)$$\n$$V=9x-y=N(9(3)-6,81(1)+9)=N(21,90).$$\nBy definition,\n$$Z=\\frac{u-9}{\\sqrt{10}},\\quad Z=\\frac{k-9}{\\sqrt{10}}=z.$$\nAgain\n$$z=\\frac{v-21}{\\sqrt{90}},\\quad Z=\\frac{2k-21}{\\sqrt{90}}=z_1.$$\nGiven $P(x+y\\le k)=P(9x-y\\ge2k)$,\n$$P(z\\le z_1)=P(z\\ge z_2)=P(z\\le-z_2).$$\nThus\n$$\\frac{k-9}{\\sqrt{10}}=-\\frac{2k-21}{\\sqrt{90}}.$$\nSolving gives $5k=48$, hence $k=\\frac{48}{5}=9.6$.\nChoice (B)",
      "status": "confirmed"
    },
